WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.7 · Financial Management
Management accounts and budget versus actual

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Compare actual with budget every month to see quickly which first-year assumptions were wrong.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.281
Management accounts are short internal reports, monthly or quarterly, that compare actual income and spending with your budget. They exist to help you decide, so on time and simple beats perfect.

4
00:00:26.681 --> 00:00:27.281
From numbers to decisions

5
00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:38.981
Budget versus actual: For each line, the budget to date, the actual to date and the difference (the variance). For example: Materials budget 600, actual 850: a variance of 250 over budget.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:49.200
Variance explained: One sentence for each big difference: timing, price or volume. For example: Volume: the youth club ran six sessions, not four, because demand was higher.

7
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:58.307
Decision: Accept the variance, correct it or change the forecast. For example: The founders accept the extra sessions and raise the materials forecast.

8
00:00:58.307 --> 00:01:10.096
Year-end forecast: Where you will end up, and whether that is acceptable. For example: At this rate the year ends 400 short, so the founders delay one purchase.

9
00:01:10.496 --> 00:01:11.096
Why the monthly check matters

10
00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:17.141
1. Your first budget is mostly assumptions; this shows quickly which were wrong.

11
00:01:17.141 --> 00:01:23.185
2. It turns numbers into decisions while there is still time to act.

12
00:01:23.185 --> 00:01:29.859
3. The routine builds the habit funders and a future board expect.

13
00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:30.859
What they feed

14
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:35.422
1. Your monthly finance check, even between two co-founders.

15
00:01:35.422 --> 00:01:39.985
2. Your cash flow forecast and next year’s budget.

16
00:01:39.985 --> 00:01:45.919
3. Your reports to funders and, later, to a board.

17
00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:46.919
What to avoid

18
00:01:46.919 --> 00:01:52.963
Waiting for perfect figures, so the report arrives too late to help.

19
00:01:52.963 --> 00:01:57.526
Listing variances without explaining them or deciding anything.

20
00:01:57.526 --> 00:02:04.941
Asking only “where are we now?” and never “where will we end up?”

21
00:02:05.541 --> 00:02:07.341
You design your first report and the review routine you start with.

22
00:02:07.341 --> 00:02:11.341
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:15.941
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
